The '''1996 African Cup of Nations''', also known as the [[Coca-Cola]] 1996 Africa Cup of Nations for sponsorship reasons, was the 20th edition of the [[Africa Cup of Nations]], the [[association football|football]] championship of Africa ([[Confederation of African Football|CAF]]). It was hosted by South Africa, who replaced original hosts Kenya. The field expanded for the first time to 16 teams, split into four groups of four; the top two teams in each group advancing to the quarterfinals. However, [[Nigeria national football team|Nigeria]] withdrew from the tournament at the final moment under pressure from then-dictator [[Sani Abacha]], reducing the field to 15.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/nigerian-players-back-boycott-of-finals-1323344.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/nigerian-players-back-boycott-of-finals-1323344.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live|title = Nigerian players back boycott of finals|website = [[Independent.co.uk]]|date = 10 January 1996}}{{cbignore}}</ref> [[South Africa national soccer team|South Africa]] won its first championship, beating [[Tunisia national football team|Tunisia]] in the final 2–0.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/60083320|title='South Africa's Rugby World Cup win was big – but this was even bigger'|work=BBC Sport |date=3 February 2022 }}</ref> The tournament was covered in the 2026 documentary series “Class of ‘96: Rise of a Nation”.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.etv.co.za/scoop/introducing-class-96-rise-nation|title='Introducing Class Of 96: Rise Of A Nation'|work=e.tv |date=7 January 2026 }}</ref>

